    "evidence_text": "However, no systematic study has previously tested this hypothesis in whole animals.\n\nAgDscam responds to infection by producing pathogen challenge-specific splice form repertoires.\n\nAgDscam is a hypervariable PRR of the A. gambiae innate immune system.\n\nDifferential innate immune response programs in neuronal subtypes determine susceptibility to infection in the brain by positive stranded RNA viruses\n\nAlphavirus-derived small RNAs modulate pathogenesis in disease vector mosquitoes.\n\nMosquito-borne viruses cause significant levels of morbidity and mortality in humans and domesticated animals.\n\nMaintenance of mosquito-borne viruses in nature requires a biological transmission cycle that involves alternating virus replication in a susceptible vertebrate and mosquito host.\n\nAn antiviral RNAi response has been shown to limit the replication of RNA viruses in flies.\n\nHowever, the importance of the RNAi pathway as an antiviral defense in mammals is unclear.\n\nDifferences in the immune responses of mammals and mosquitoes may explain why these viruses are not generally associated with pathology in the invertebrate host.\n\nviRNAs had an asymmetric distribution that spanned the length of the SINV genome.\n\nDecreased survival was observed in mosquitoes in which the accumulation of viRNAs was suppressed.\n\nBeta interferon controls West Nile virus infection and pathogenesis in mice.\n\nHowever, the specific functions of individual IFN subtypes remain uncertain.\n\nThe increased susceptibility of IFN-\u03b2(-)(/)(-) mice was accompanied by enhanced viral replication in different tissues.\n\nA C-Type Lectin Collaborates with a CD45 Phosphatase Homolog to Facilitate West Nile Virus Infection of Mosquitoes\n\nIn vivo experiments show that mosGCTL-1 and mosPTP-1 function as part of the same pathway and are critical for WNV infection of mosquitoes.\n\nDuring the mosquito blood-feeding process, WNV infection was blocked in vivo with mosGCTL-1 antibodies.\n\nA molecular understanding of flaviviral-arthropod interactions may lead to strategies to control viral dissemination in nature.\n\nViral Paratransgenesis in the Malaria Vector Anopheles gambiae\n\nHowever, An.\n\ngambiae has been shown to be refractory to DNV dissemination.\n\ngambiae.\n\nWe developed a flexible AgDNV-based expression vector to express any gene of interest in An.\n\ngambiae using a two-plasmid helper-transducer system.\n\ngambiae mosquitoes.\n\nWild-type and EGFP-transducing AgDNV virions were highly infectious to An.\n\ngambiae.\n\nThe myeloid transcription factor GATA-2 regulates the viral UL144 gene during human cytomegalovirus latency in an isolate-specific manner.\n\nGenetic variation in West Nile virus from naturally infected mosquitoes and birds suggests quasispecies structure and strong purifying selection.\n\nResults from this analysis demonstrate that WNV infections are derived from a genetically diverse population of genomes in nature.\n\nWNV sequences in mosquitoes were significantly more genetically diverse than WNV in birds.\n\nEvidence of purifying selection was obtained from both intra- and interhost WNV populations.\n\nWest Nile virus (WNV) is a major cause of mosquito-borne illness in the United States.\n\nHuman disease ranges from mild febrile illness to severe fatal neurologic infection.",
